MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hey all!

I wanted to clarify how the "warm" parameter works in the micro_era5/etc functions. I see in the code that it alters the air temperatures; that carries through to everything else, right? Meaning, soil temperature will also increase because it's dependent on what the air temperature is, etc. Or does "warm" just influence air temperature and leave everything else alone?

Best
Adam

From u6cec8ea3702ece93 at groups.google.com  Tue Dec  7 03:18:26 2021
From: u6cec8ea3702ece93 at groups.google.com (NicheMapR)
Date: Tue, 07 Dec 2021 03:18:26 +0000
Subject: Re: Possible to estimate water temperature from a microclimate model?
Message-ID: <UrXuQAFKIGI.NgkRCG0bBQAJ@nichemapr.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <UrXuQAFKIGI.EyNYzbC4BgAJ@nichemapr.groups.google.com>
References: <UrXuQAFKIGI.EyNYzbC4BgAJ@nichemapr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/nichemapr/c/UrXuQAFKIGI/m/NgkRCG0bBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Nick,

Check out a previous post on this form March this year under the title "simulating pond temperatures". There are two ways to roughly simulate a pool of water with NicheMapR. One is as you suggested, where you set the soil to be high in clay, and you can also set a multiplier on the rainfall to simulate a catchment (rainmult parameter). I've found this to work very well in simulating wetland dynamics in terms of water depth. There is no calculation of temperature here though. You could get a rough idea of this by simulating water-logged soil of low bulk density and seeing what that gives you at different depths. The other approach is to use the container model of the ectotherm model which will give you a water temperature (for the 'core' of the pool).

Re simulating climate change effects, to do this you need to feed the microclimate model the future climate change scenario conditions.

All the best,
Mike

